        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Given the level of fidelity of the National Association of Stock Car Auto Racing (NASCAR) data and thorough documentation of injuries, there should be a strong correlation between driver injury and biodynamic response. Additionally, since NASCAR shares more similarities with Orion (NASA's Multi-Purpose Crew Vehicle) than do passenger car occupant protection systems, the probability of injury determined from NASCAR would be better correlated to actual injury risk for Orion.
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
Via a Space Act Agreement, the Occupant Protection (OP) team obtained data on race car crash event dynamics and associated injuries for all recorded impacts during the 2002 to 2008 racing seasons, including impacts during race events, qualifying heats, and practice. The data included detailed information about resulting injuries (if any) and associated abbreviated injury scale (AIS) ratings, including any intrusion into the occupant compartment, which could affect the relevance and magnitude of recorded injuries and accelerations. NASCAR provided 4015 non-attributable impact files, including 44 with injury information. Vehicle dynamics were recorded on each vehicle when acceleration exceeded 4.5 G.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
The processed data were analyzed to calculate peak acceleration, change in velocity, pulse length, and other appropriate metrics. Dynamic Finite Element (FE) analysis software LS-DYNA (Livermore Software Technology Corporation 2009) was used with a 50th percentile male Hybrid III Anthropomorphic Test Device (ATD) finite element model (FEM) developed and validated by Humanetics Innovative Solutions, Inc. The FEM configured a NASCAR seat (generalized representation) with head and neck restraint device, 5-point harness, helmet, and head surround.
<p>
        </div>

